// The camera rig: a fixed tilt-and-pull-back, the same "translate * rotate"
// shape demos/gl3dgame/world/scene/render/init.id's build_view uses --
// tilt the disk 55 degrees so the spiral reads as a disk seen from above
// rather than edge-on, then push the whole thing 12 units down -Z so the
// (up to 5-unit-radius, see conf.id's maximum_radius) cloud
// sits well inside the frustum. camera_proj rebuilds the perspective
// matrix from gl_aspect_x1000() every single frame (rather than caching it
// once like demos/gl3d does) specifically to track a live window resize,
// per backends/gl/README.md.

// gl_mat_*'s return is an opaque slot in the GL backend's matrix pool
// (backends/gl/gl_linux.c's mat_alloc), not a computed value, so these are
// pinned against what a fresh process's first allocations actually come
// back as, the same way demos/gl3d/scene/render/frame.id's modelview and
// compose_rotation are.
camera_rig() {
  int camera_translation = gl_mat_translate(0, 0, 0 - 12000);
  int camera_tilt = gl_mat_rotate_x(55000);
  int camera_matrix = gl_mat_mul(camera_translation, camera_tilt);
} return int camera_matrix;
():(2)
